3
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Many Americans see this as the most consequential election in recent American history. Amid the backdrop of a pandemic, a season of racial unrest, a suddenly open Supreme Court seat and months of natural disasters fueled by climate change. What will shape voters' decisions this year? What are the psychological forces at play when people decide to vote and whom to vote for? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that explores the connections between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills.
许多美国人认为这是美国近代史上最重要的一次选举。背景是全球大流行、种族动荡时期、最高法院席位突然空缺，以及气候变化引发的数月自然灾害。今年是什么在影响选民的决定？当人们决定投票以及投给谁时，背后有哪些心理力量在起作用？欢迎收听美国心理学会的旗舰播客《心理学谈话》，该节目旨在探索心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯。

4
00:01:22.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Our guest today is Dr. John Krosnick, a social psychologist who directs the political, political psychology research group at Stanford University. He has spent 30 years studying Americans' political attitudes, what drives them to turn out for elections, and how their political attitudes shape their voting choices. In recent years, he's had a particular interest in climate change attitudes, which Americans say is one of the most critical issues we are facing. Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, Dr. Krosnick. Thank you so much for having me. It's the pleasure to be here. Right now, polls are showing Joe Biden ahead of Donald Trump in many battleground states. You've done research on polling and survey methodology. How seriously should voters take these polls right now?
我们今天的嘉宾是约翰·克罗斯尼克博士，他是一位社会心理学家，负责斯坦福大学政治心理学研究小组。他花了 30 年时间研究美国人的政治态度、是什么促使他们参加选举，以及他们的政治态度如何塑造他们的投票选择。近年来，他特别关注气候变化态度，美国人称这是我们面临的最关键问题之一。欢迎来到《心理学谈话》，克罗斯尼克博士。非常感谢您的邀请。很高兴来到这里。目前，民意调查显示乔·拜登在许多摇摆州领先于唐纳德·特朗普。您在民意调查和调查方法方面做过研究。选民现在应该多认真地看待这些民调？

5
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Well, it's a wonderful question, and it's one that I'm very glad that the country is interested in. Unfortunately, a memory many people have about the polls is that in 2016, many people went to bed the night before the election believing that from the poll aggregators 538.com, the upshot at the New York Times, and others, that Hillary Clinton had a probability of winning that election somewhere between the high 60s and the 90s. And when, in fact, she didn't win, of course, it was a historic moment for the country, but it was also an important moment for social science because the sense in which the polls are broken and perhaps hopelessly broken was certainly an appealing interpretation to many
嗯，这是一个非常好的问题，我也很高兴全国人民都对此感兴趣。不幸的是，许多人对民调的记忆是 2016 年，当时许多人在选举前一天晚上睡觉时，根据民调汇总机构 538.com、《纽约时报》的“上策”栏目以及其他机构的预测，认为希拉里·克林顿赢得选举的概率在 60% 到 90% 之间。而事实上，她并没有获胜，这当然是美国历史上的一个重要时刻，但对社会科学来说也是一个重要时刻，因为民调已经崩溃，甚至可能无可救药地崩溃，这种感觉对许多人来说确实是一种很有吸引力的解释。

6
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:47.000
people. But I'm happy to say that that is wrong. that the polls, it's important to differentiate two kinds of surveys, the scientific ones and the not-so-scientific ones. Scientific surveys involve truly random samples of people. And that methodology done with what's called random digit dialing, where we generate random numbers to call all possible landlines and cell phones with known probabilities, that method has produced startlingly accurate pre-election predictions for, decades. And in fact, that method worked beautifully in 2016. The polls done during the last week before the election, either national polls or state polls, predicted the outcomes for President Trump and for Secretary Clinton with about one percentage point of error, just about exactly
但我很高兴地说，那是错误的。重要的是要区分两种调查：科学的调查和不那么科学的调查。科学调查涉及真正随机的人群样本。这种使用所谓“随机数字拨号”的方法，即我们生成随机号码来拨打所有可能的固定电话和手机，并具有已知的概率，这种方法几十年来一直产生惊人准确的选前预测。事实上，这种方法在 2016 年运作得非常完美。选举前最后一周进行的民调，无论是全国民调还是州民调，对特朗普总统和克林顿国务卿的选举结果预测误差仅在 1 个百分点左右，几乎完全正确。

7
00:03:47.000 --> 00:04:35.000
right. The unfortunate fact is that there were many, many more non-scientific polls that involved not random samples, and instead samples of people who either volunteer to do surveys regularly for money or samples of people who were scooped up by a method called river sampling. And so these are people who are doing something on the internet and, for example, want to read a newspaper article and a box pops up that says, before you can read the article, you need to answer this question, who you're going to vote for on Tuesday. There's nothing scientific about those samples. There's nothing random about those samples. Unfortunately, companies are selling those samples as if they are scientific, and those are the samples that were way off with errors as big as 16 percentage
不幸的事实是，还有许多非科学的民调，它们不涉及随机样本，而是涉及那些为了钱定期自愿参加调查的人，或者是通过一种叫“河流抽样”的方法收集的人。这些人正在互联网上做某事，例如想阅读一篇报纸文章，然后弹出一个框说，在阅读文章之前，您需要回答这个问题：您周二打算投给谁？这些样本没有任何科学性可言。这些样本没有任何随机性。不幸的是，一些公司正在把这些样本当作科学样本来出售，而这些样本的误差高达 16 个百分点，考虑到其背后没有任何科学依据，这也就不足为奇了。

8
00:04:35.000 --> 00:05:21.000
points off, not surprising given that there was no science behind it. So the answer is that happily, I think many news organizations learned that lesson, and for many of us in 2020, we are watching the polls more often the ones getting high visibility, not always, but the ones getting high visibility so far are the scientific random sample polls. And unfortunately, you know, for your listeners, the challenge is when you see a poll, how can you decide whether it's one that you should trust or not? And of course, I know the survey organizations. And so when I see when I know what methods they use, and so I can tell, and I would like it if the news media would tell everybody when they report on a survey, whether it's a truly random sample or not. And if you care,
所以答案是，令人高兴的是，我认为许多新闻机构吸取了教训。对于我们许多人来说，在 2020 年，我们更频繁地关注民调，那些获得高曝光度的民调——并非总是如此，但到目前为止获得高曝光度的民调——都是科学的随机样本民调。不幸的是，对于您的听众来说，挑战在于当您看到一个民调时，如何判断它是否值得信任？当然，我了解这些调查机构。所以当我看到并知道他们使用什么方法时，我就能分辨出来。我希望新闻媒体在报道调查时能告诉大家，这是否是一个真正的随机样本。如果您关心的话，

9
00:05:21.000 --> 00:06:09.000
take a minute and click some links to follow through to find a methodology right up. And if you find the description is a random digit dial by telephone, by human interviewers to landlines and cell phones, that's the critical factor. And so the good news is by careful sampling, we can still learn. And I hope before this election that there will be not only lots of scientific national polls, but lots of scientific state polls so that we all have a good sense of what to expect. when the votes are counted. And as I say, there is a track record that goes back many decades showing we should have strong expectations based on scientific polls. So it's not necessarily a brand or a name that the public should be looking for, although that may be part of it, like whether you look at a Gallup poll or National
花一分钟点击一些链接，查看方法论说明。如果您发现描述是“由人工访谈员通过电话对固定电话和手机进行随机数字拨号”，这就是关键因素。好消息是，通过仔细的抽样，我们仍然可以了解情况。我希望在这次选举之前，不仅会有大量的全国性科学民调，还会有大量的州级科学民调，这样我们大家在计票时都能对结果有很好的预判。正如我所说，几十年的记录表明，我们应该基于科学民调有很强的预期。所以公众不一定非要盯着某个品牌或名字，尽管这可能是一部分，比如您看盖洛普民调或芝加哥大学的国家民意研究中心，但真正受过教育的消费者应该关注方法论。这就是诀窍。

10
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Opinion Research Center out of the University of Chicago, for example, but that really educated consumers should be looking at the methodology. That's the trick. Yeah. And luckily, there's only a little bit of the, the methodology to look for. This pretty much differentiates the pros from not. Random sampling, random digit dial, RDD, it's called telephone, human interviewers, landline cell phones. And I can tell you that, you know, there are certain organizations. You mentioned Gallup. Gallup stopped doing pre-election polls. I'm not aware that they're doing them now. But the Pew Research Center is doing them. They're very reliable. ABC News is another example of a very reliable organization.
是的。幸运的是，需要关注的方法论只有一点点。这基本上区分了专业人士和非专业人士。随机抽样、随机数字拨号（RDD）、电话、人工访谈员、固定电话和手机。我可以告诉您，有一些机构。您提到了盖洛普。盖洛普停止了选前民调。我不确定他们现在是否在做。但皮尤研究中心正在做。他们非常可靠。美国广播公司新闻部是另一个非常可靠的机构的例子。

11
00:06:50.000 --> 00:07:18.000
And there are others as well. Kwynipiac University is another one that partners, I think, maybe with the Washington Post. That's another one using random digital. And there are others as well. You published a review study called Why Do People Vote? And one thing you found is that door-to-door canvassing is one of the most effective ways to increase voter turnout, whereas phone calls seem to make no difference. Can you talk about that research and how that might be playing out this year?
还有其他一些机构。昆尼皮亚克大学是另一个合作伙伴，我想可能与《华盛顿邮报》合作。这也是另一个使用随机数字拨号的机构。还有其他一些。您发表了一项名为《人们为什么投票？》的综述研究。您发现的一件事是，挨家挨户的拉票是提高选民投票率最有效的方法之一，而电话似乎没有任何区别。您能谈谈这项研究以及它今年可能如何发挥作用吗？

12
00:07:18.000 --> 00:08:09.000
Yes, really many people think of Don Green, who is a political scientist at Columbia University, as a really important contributor to our understanding of voter mobilization. Because for many decades in America, it's been big business at the time of elections for candidates to pay for television advertising, to pay for radio advertising, to mail out postcards and letters to voters in the hopes of influencing their behavior. And yet, up until Don started to study all of this, we had very little evidence. And Don himself was one of the first people to test the impact of television advertising of candidates and to show really tiny effects of ads that dissipate so quickly they can't possibly influence Election Day.
是的，确实很多人认为哥伦比亚大学的政治学家唐·格林是我们理解选民动员的重要贡献者。因为几十年来，在美国，候选人为电视广告付费、为广播广告付费、向选民邮寄明信片和信件以期影响他们的行为，这在大选时是一项大生意。然而，在唐开始研究这一切之前，我们几乎没有什么证据。唐本人是第一批测试候选人电视广告影响的人之一，他证明了广告的效果非常微小，而且消散得如此之快，以至于它们不可能影响选举日。

13
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:56.000
And so in a sense, his message was tremendous amounts of money are being spent on ads that have no real measurable impact. and others as well, Lynn Vavrick at UCLA is another one who has done some work in this area and showed the same thing. And it's really very difficult to find any evidence that television advertising, which is quite expensive for candidates, has any consequences. So Don then turned to this question of voter mobilization. And the logic really comes from the following, that for a long time, candidates thought that whether you are going to vote or not is pretty much a fixed attribute of you and has nothing. to do with them. And that you're, so either you're a voter or you're not. And the real question is, can they convert you to vote for them instead of for their
所以从某种意义上说，他的信息是：大量的金钱被花在了没有真正可衡量影响的广告上。其他人也是如此，加州大学洛杉矶分校的琳恩·瓦夫里克也在这方面做了一些工作，并显示了同样的结果。确实很难找到任何证据表明对候选人来说相当昂贵的电视广告有任何后果。所以唐随后转向了选民动员的问题。逻辑实际上来自以下几点：长期以来，候选人认为您是否投票很大程度上是您的固定属性，与他们无关。所以要么您是选民，要么您不是。真正的问题是，他们能否让您转而投给他们而不是他们的对手？

14
00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:36.000
opponent? And in recent years, the professionals working in this area became much more enlightened by realizing that actually it's quite hard to convince people to move from one side to the other in terms of candidates. But it is much easier to activate a person who probably. will vote but might not vote into actually voting. So imagine that you have kind of a 70% background probability of voting that giving you a bit of a nudge can make a significant difference. And if before I give you that nudge, I know that if I can nudge you into voting, you'll vote for me.
近年来，在这一领域工作的专业人士变得更加开明，他们意识到实际上很难说服人们在候选人方面从一边倒向另一边。但激活一个可能投票但可能不会投票的人去实际投票要容易得多。所以想象一下，您有 70% 的投票背景概率，给我一点推动力可能会产生重大影响。如果在我给您那个推动力之前，我知道如果我能推动您去投票，您就会投给我。

15
00:09:36.000 --> 00:10:19.000
Then that's a good thing. And so the Obama campaign is widely credited in 2008 with really paying attention to Don's research and realizing that it made sense on a scientific and an empirical basis to deploy resources to try to figure out who would vote for Obama if they vote and to figure out who to nudge to have the most impact on them. And what Don's field experiments showed was that mailing out postcards to people has very little, if any, effect. Making phone calls to people has very little, if any, effect. But having the right conversation on the doorstep actually produced quite measurable effects, increasing turnout.
那是一件好事。因此，奥巴马竞选团队在 2008 年被广泛认为真正关注了唐的研究，并意识到在科学和经验的基础上配置资源来弄清楚谁会在投票时投给奥巴马，以及弄清楚推动谁能产生最大的影响，这是有道理的。唐的实地实验表明，给人们邮寄明信片几乎没有什么效果，如果有的话。给人们打电话几乎没有什么效果，如果有的话。但在门口进行正确的对话实际上产生了相当可衡量的效果，提高了投票率。

16
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:58.000
And so for psychologists, we ask the question, why? What is it that happens during that face-to-face conversation that causes an increase in turnout? And in fact, we've known for quite a while from Tony Greenwald, who's at the University of Washington and his research, where way ahead of the curve, he realized that it was possible during a conversation like that with a person to simply ask one question on election day, are you going to vote? And asking that question is enough of a nudge to actually alter behavior.
所以对于心理学家来说，我们问的问题是，为什么？在那次面对面的谈话中发生了什么导致了投票率的增加？事实上，我们从华盛顿大学的托尼·格林沃尔德及其研究中已经知道了一段时间，他走在时代前沿，他意识到在与人进行那样的谈话时，可以在选举日简单地问一个问题：您打算投票吗？问那个问题足以作为一个推动力来实际改变行为。

17
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:44.000
And I'll tell you about the studies that he did. What he did was to randomly assign households that were listed in the state of Washington official government records as being registered to vote. He had undergraduates at the University of Washington, I think, make phone calls to those randomly selected households for which he could get phone numbers. And each household was randomly assigned to get one of the following two quick conversations. Hi, I'm an undergraduate at the University of Washington doing a survey in the psychology department. It's just one question today. Are you going to vote in the election on Tuesday or who do you think is going to win the Seattle Seahawks football game on Sunday? And so each person's randomly assigned either to get that vote question or to get the football question.
我会告诉您他所做的研究。他所做的是随机分配那些在华盛顿州官方政府记录中登记投票的家庭。我想他让华盛顿大学的本科生给那些他能获得电话号码的随机选择的家庭打电话。每个家庭被随机分配进行以下两段简短对话中的一段。您好，我是华盛顿大学心理学系的本科生，正在进行一项调查。今天只有一个问题。您打算在周二的选举中投票吗？或者您认为谁会赢得周日的西雅图海鹰队橄榄球比赛？所以每个人都被随机分配要么回答那个投票问题，要么回答那个橄榄球问题。

18
00:11:44.000 --> 00:12:42.000
And the evidence that Tony provided from a series of studies is that simply asking that question, are you going to vote, actually increases turnout because there are people who kind of lean in the direction of voting. And when they hear themselves say, yeah, I'm going to vote, it actually changes their self-concept and creates a little bit more crystallization of a behavioral intention. And so that same logic, I think, makes sense and applies on doorsteps. And so that turns out to be a technique that was used in 2008 and in subsequent elections. And as you know, 2016 allowed it to happen. 2020 is not so much. The idea of going around and knocking on doors and talking face to face with people is unimaginable for most. And so that's one of them, so many things that makes this a remarkably different delection from what we've been used to.
托尼从一系列研究中提供的证据是，简单地问“您打算投票吗”这个问题，实际上增加了投票率，因为有些人倾向于投票。当他们听到自己说“是的，我要去投票”时，它实际上改变了他们的自我概念，并使行为意图更加具体化。所以我认为同样的逻辑在门口也是合理的，也是适用的。事实证明，这是一种在 2008 年和随后的选举中使用的技术。如您所知，2016 年允许它发生。2020 年则不然。到处敲门并与人面对面交谈的想法对大多数人来说是不可想象的。所以这是让这次选举与我们习惯的选举截然不同的众多事情之一。

19
00:12:42.000 --> 00:13:31.000
And I think many people who want to make a difference are volunteering to make phone calls. And it may make the phone callers feel better to make the phone calls. But at least Don's evidence is that not seeing somebody, not talking to somebody who you can connect with on the doorstep and answering that question freely, you know, at least in his studies, didn't work. but we should remember in Tony Greenwald's studies, it did work to call people on the phone and simply ask that question. So maybe we shouldn't completely abandon that notion in 2020. Given that the research shows that advertising and postcards and some of these phone calls are not very effective, why do campaigns continue to spend money on them? Such a wonderful question.
我认为许多想有所作为的人正在志愿打电话。打电话可能会让打电话的人感觉更好。但至少唐的证据是，看不到某人，不与您可以在门口联系并自由回答那个问题的人交谈，至少在他的研究中，并没有奏效。但我们应该记住，在托尼·格林沃尔德的研究中，打电话给人们并简单地问那个问题确实奏效了。所以也许我们不应该在 2020 年完全放弃这个概念。鉴于研究表明广告、明信片和其中一些电话效果不佳，为什么竞选团队继续在上面花钱？真是个好问题。

20
00:13:31.000 --> 00:14:25.000
Seems logical to me. I'm sorry to tell you there are so many instances in which I am amazed that business spends money in ways that don't work, that government does things that the public doesn't want it to do. And one potential answer to your question is that they simply aren't aware of the research. If the research is being done, it's being published in academic journals, it's certainly known. in some circles, but maybe not in their circles. I'll tell you a story, though, that gave me a little bit of insight in general into this. I was lucky to be able to collaborate with some folks at the University of Chicago at their public policy school in sponsoring a series of workshops, wonderful workshops, where they brought together political psychologists, political
